WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.244
Durable secrets management for .

2
00:00:02.244 --> 00:00:06.031
NET applications is a repeatable engineering workflow.

3
00:00:06.031 --> 00:00:10.029
Start by identifying secrets and sensitive configuration.

4
00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:17.813
Inventory each value by owner, purpose, environment, access path, rotation expectation, and operational impact.

5
00:00:17.813 --> 00:00:28.123
Keep secret values out of source code, ordinary appsettings files, logs, build artifacts, screenshots, support bundles, tickets, and chat messages.

6
00:00:28.123 --> 00:00:40.466
Use harmless placeholders such as ExampleApiKey or ExampleConnectionString in documentation and examples, and keep real values in systems designed for secret lifecycle control.

7
00:00:40.466 --> 00:00:44.183
Use appropriate secret stores and runtime identities.

8
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:59.261
Centralize retrieval logic, validate required configuration without printing values, handle missing secrets safely, and use least privilege for applications, developers, operators, pipelines, and service identities.

9
00:00:59.261 --> 00:01:00.874
Maintain the lifecycle.

10
00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:14.269
Plan rotation before emergencies, revoke exposed values promptly, remove old credentials from dependent systems, audit secret access, monitor unusual patterns, and review access periodically.

11
00:01:14.269 --> 00:01:18.267
Secret safety decays when ownership and review disappear.

12
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:24.649
The practical message is straightforward: secrets are part of the application architecture.

13
00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:28.436
Treat them as owned, reviewed, monitored dependencies.

14
00:01:28.436 --> 00:01:32.293
When teams make secret handling ordinary and durable, .

15
00:01:32.293 --> 00:01:36.571
NET applications become easier to operate securely over time.
